Not exactly.  The steam engine analogy has two main faults: these days
there aren't too many people who have a good working knowledge of
steam engines because they haven't been in common use for a century or
so (which isn't to say that a modern engineer wouldn't understand the
theory; just that he wouldn't have developed an intuition about them.)
Whereas there are plenty of people left who knew pre-ANSI C when it
was current, and to whom it's second nature.  Besides, the differences
aren't *that* significant; pre-ANSI just has a weird way of declaring
functions and is missing a couple of keywords.  I learned C about 8
years ago, well after standardization, and I can understand pre-ANSI
just fine as long as you don't ask me anything too arcane.

But the main thing is that unlike the difference between e.g. steam
power and nuclear power, it isn't that the modern techniques are
inherently more powerful; they just have different aesthetics.

Personally, I'd rather deal with a function that's 200 lines long and
references a few globals than a function that's only a wrapper around
another function...that's inherited from a function in a superclass...
that's *also* inherited...from a function that's a wrapper around
another function...that... (And I've *seen* code like this.)  With
Nethack's code, everything I need to know to understand a function is
right there in the same source file.

The variants weren't created because people were afraid to tinker with
the code unnecessarily; they were created when people *did* tinker
with the code unnecessarily.  Likewise the 279 patches at bilious.
People have been tinkering with the Nethack code since it was called
Hack, and they won't be stopping any time soon.


